Microsoft Learn: Configure Network Security Groups

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Configure Network Security Groups
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to implement and manage Azure Network Security Groups (NSGs) to control inbound and outbound network traffic, secure Azure resources, and enforce network access policies.

This module focused on creating and managing Network Security Groups, understanding NSG rules and rule processing, implementing Application Security Groups, and verifying effective security configurations across Azure virtual networks.

Step 1: Understanding Network Security Groups (NSGs)

The module introduced Network Security Groups (NSGs) as Azure's primary network filtering and traffic control mechanism.

An NSG acts as a virtual firewall that controls network traffic entering and leaving Azure resources.

Resources commonly protected by NSGs:
Virtual Machines
Network Interfaces (NICs)
Subnets
Application workloads
Key benefits:
Network traffic filtering
Resource protection
Access control
Segmentation of workloads
Improved security posture

The module emphasized that NSGs are one of the most important Azure networking security controls.

Step 2: Implementing Network Security Groups

The training covered how NSGs are deployed and associated with Azure resources.

NSGs can be assigned to:
Assignment Type	Purpose
Subnet	Applies rules to all resources in the subnet
Network Interface (NIC)	Applies rules to a specific VM or resource
Benefits:
Flexible security control
Centralized management
Granular traffic filtering
Key concepts learned:
NSGs can secure entire subnets
NSGs can secure individual virtual machines
Multiple layers of protection can be applied

The module demonstrated how NSGs create security boundaries within Azure networks.

Step 3: Understanding NSG Rules

A major focus of the module was understanding Network Security Group rules.

NSG rules determine whether network traffic is allowed or denied.

Rule components:
Component	Purpose
Priority	Rule evaluation order
Source	Traffic origin
Destination	Traffic target
Protocol	TCP, UDP, or Any
Port	Network port
Action	Allow or Deny
Example Rule:
Priority: 100
Source: Internet
Destination: VM
Port: 443
Action: Allow

This allows HTTPS traffic from the internet to a virtual machine.

Step 4: Inbound and Outbound Rules

The training covered both inbound and outbound traffic filtering.

Inbound Rules

Control traffic entering a resource.

Examples:

Allow HTTPS (443)
Allow RDP (3389)
Allow SSH (22)
Outbound Rules

Control traffic leaving a resource.

Examples:

Allow internet access
Restrict communication with external networks
Limit application connectivity
Key lessons learned:
NSGs evaluate inbound and outbound traffic separately
Both directions require appropriate rule configurations
Step 5: Understanding Rule Priorities

The module emphasized the importance of rule priorities.

Rule Processing

Azure evaluates NSG rules from the lowest priority number to the highest.

Example:

Priority	Action
100	Allow HTTPS
200	Allow RDP
300	Deny All

Azure stops processing after the first matching rule.

Key concepts learned:
Lower numbers have higher priority
First matching rule wins
Proper rule ordering is critical

The module highlighted rule priority as a frequently tested AZ-104 topic.

Step 6: Default Security Rules

The training introduced built-in NSG rules automatically provided by Azure.

Examples of default rules:
Rule	Purpose
AllowVNetInBound	Allows traffic within the VNet
AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound	Supports Azure Load Balancer traffic
DenyAllInbound	Blocks all other inbound traffic
AllowVNetOutBound	Allows outbound VNet traffic
AllowInternetOutBound	Allows internet access
DenyAllOutbound	Blocks remaining outbound traffic
Key lessons learned:
Default rules cannot be deleted
Custom rules can override defaults through priority settings
Understanding default rules is essential for troubleshooting
Step 7: Understanding Effective Security Rules

A major focus of the module was determining effective rules.

Effective rules represent the final result of all security rules applied to a resource.

Sources of effective rules:
Subnet NSGs
Network Interface NSGs
Default rules
Custom rules
Benefits:
Simplified troubleshooting
Security validation
Visibility into final traffic behavior
Key concepts learned:
Multiple NSGs can influence traffic
Effective rules show actual enforcement results
Administrators should verify effective rules during troubleshooting
Step 8: Creating Custom NSG Rules

The module demonstrated creating custom security rules.

Common use cases:
Allow HTTPS traffic
Allow SQL Server communication
Restrict administrative access
Block unauthorized traffic
Administrative tasks practiced:
Defining source and destination
Selecting protocols
Configuring ports
Assigning priorities
Choosing allow or deny actions

The module emphasized least-privilege networking principles.

Step 9: Implementing Application Security Groups (ASGs)

The training introduced Application Security Groups (ASGs).

Application Security Groups allow administrators to group virtual machines by application function rather than IP address.

Example:
Web Servers
App Servers
Database Servers
Benefits:
Simplified rule management
Easier scalability
Reduced administrative complexity
Application-focused security
Example Rule:
Allow:
Web Servers → App Servers

Allow:
App Servers → Database Servers
Key lessons learned:
ASGs reduce dependence on IP-based rules
Security policies become easier to manage
ASGs support dynamic cloud environments

The module demonstrated how ASGs improve network security management at scale.

